{"h":3395,"w":2400,"lines":[{"t":"Test 6","c":1,"h":0.014002843796153797,"x":0.094460853743544418,"y":0.072213694359931302,"w":0.062107098897298166},{"y":0.1177325579753602,"t":"READING PASSAGE 3","w":0.31069959004720055,"h":0.015988372380376759,"c":1,"x":0.094650205158419376},{"x":0.09670781171966325,"y":0.15400471130280735,"h":0.017506916756062751,"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 27-40, which are based on Reading","w":0.8251028951009115,"c":1},{"c":1,"x":0.094650204694712511,"y":0.17432273270593779,"w":0.17283950805664061,"h":0.016195524146054296,"t":"Passage 3 below."},{"y":0.21075581376361019,"x":0.29835391976774589,"h":0.02039848592733462,"c":1,"w":0.43209874471028648,"t":"The Benefits of Being Bilingual"},{"y":0.247692312014821,"x":0.094570822035266719,"w":0.83968419392903637,"h":0.020794936990007784,"c":1,"t":"A According to the latest figures, the majority of the world's population is now bilingual"},{"w":0.78395060221354163,"x":0.14197531627135504,"y":0.26737338053230775,"h":0.017510340547954639,"t":"or multilingual, having grown up speaking two or more languages. In the past, such","c":1},{"c":1,"t":"children were considered to be at a disadvantage compared with their monolingual","w":0.7822288513183594,"h":0.023130032704491543,"y":0.28253608873492231,"x":0.14182304696431838},{"x":0.14189621651576392,"w":0.77588562011718754,"h":0.019069694096685463,"c":1,"t":"peers. Over the past few decades, however, technological advances have allowed","y":0.30215118915142902},{"t":"researchers to look more deeply at how bilingualism interacts with and changes","w":0.75332087198893238,"h":0.020792049008067304,"y":0.31839913814818344,"c":1,"x":0.14184756692037298},{"t":"the cognitive and neurological systems, thereby identifying several clear benefits of","x":0.14188562521118134,"w":0.78829279581705736,"h":0.021712232394269004,"c":1,"y":0.33733999396372549},{"c":1,"w":0.14814814249674479,"h":0.016048292108362894,"t":"being bilingual.","x":0.14197531136177038,"y":0.3589517080235578},{"w":0.73266820271809896,"h":0.019234209094367571,"x":0.14396275587682131,"y":0.38366924801361457,"t":"Research shows that when a bilingual person uses one language, the other is","c":1},{"y":0.40096407858869554,"h":0.018994625662184683,"t":"active at the same time. When we hear a word, we don't hear the entire word all at","x":0.14394922126616402,"w":0.78000096638997385,"c":1},{"y":0.41860465095154664,"t":"once: the sounds arrive in sequential order. Long before the word is finished, the","x":0.14403293461170591,"h":0.017496644818740581,"w":0.7592592366536457,"c":1},{"x":0.14393471872174188,"h":0.021634569156857797,"t":"brain's language system begins to guess what that word might be. If you hear 'can',","y":0.43441239301157242,"w":0.78822087605794267,"c":1},{"x":0.14188335753321249,"y":0.45119154465997036,"t":"you will likely activate words like 'candy' and 'candle' as well, at least during the","h":0.022677454145271891,"c":1,"w":0.75118601481119796},{"w":0.77796747843424474,"c":1,"h":0.019532438442198874,"t":"earlier stages of word recognition. For bilingual people, this activation is not limited","x":0.14394852444459777,"y":0.47211167934967779},{"t":"to a single language; auditory input activates corresponding words regardless","x":0.14403292861802905,"y":0.49116607773574739,"w":0.73251027425130211,"h":0.017667844522968212,"c":1},{"x":0.14390054958182247,"t":"of the language to which they belong. Some of the most compelling evidence","w":0.72864415486653655,"h":0.021209557289510017,"c":1,"y":0.50681559039809199},{"c":1,"w":0.74485595703125007,"y":0.5232558135162666,"h":0.020348837575025036,"x":0.14197531654610335,"t":"for this phenomenon, called 'language co-activation', comes from studying eye"},{"y":0.54117268483945469,"t":"movements. A Russian-English bilingual asked to 'pick up a marker' from a set of","x":0.14386775814973618,"w":0.76786244710286466,"c":1,"h":0.023788638614793856},{"t":"objects would look more at a stamp than someone who doesn't know Russian,","w":0.74279836018880219,"h":0.016195524146054185,"x":0.14609053195831309,"c":1,"y":0.56242638382986399},{"t":"because the Russian word for 'stamp', marka, sounds like the English word he or","h":0.019140164899882239,"x":0.14403291485775754,"w":0.76748972574869789,"c":1,"y":0.5797997645026941},{"w":0.78600824991861984,"x":0.14609052515915871,"c":1,"h":0.018929588219021465,"y":0.59738372131921536,"t":"she heard, 'marker'. In cases like this, language co-activation occurs because what"},{"c":1,"t":"the listener hears could map onto words in either language.","w":0.56584360758463537,"y":0.61627906960389023,"x":0.14403292866251122,"h":0.017441860778592888},{"w":0.018518517812093099,"y":0.64534883693333822,"t":"C","h":0.014534883982160851,"c":1,"x":0.098765432349097459},{"t":"Having to deal with this persistent linguistic competition can result in difficulties,","x":0.14403293602483663,"y":0.64244186078947874,"h":0.018924452531183245,"c":1,"w":0.75308639526367194},{"t":"however. For instance, knowing more than one language can cause speakers to","y":0.65976206072082544,"x":0.14597526963740667,"w":0.75741755167643221,"h":0.020194333349436877,"c":1},{"c":1,"t":"name pictures more slowly, and can increase 'tip-of-the-tongue states', when you","w":0.76543207804361979,"x":0.1460905507312765,"h":0.018953555885557583,"y":0.67726737376620139},{"c":1,"x":0.14609054255082715,"y":0.69611307443422266,"t":"can almost, but not quite, bring a word to mind. As a result, the constant juggling of","w":0.78395060221354163,"h":0.019140164899882239},{"x":0.146028883787925,"t":"two languages creates a need to control how much a person accesses a language","y":0.71422294580519974,"w":0.77994928995768231,"h":0.018599011451532532,"c":1},{"t":"at any given time. For this reason, bilingual people often perform better on tasks","x":0.14592701305129577,"y":0.72973329111259599,"h":0.022426124455931618,"c":1,"w":0.75750066121419268},{"h":0.017441860778592888,"x":0.14609054727842932,"t":"that require conflict management. In the classic Stroop Task, people see a word","y":0.74999999983720556,"w":0.75308639526367194,"c":1},{"w":0.73251027425130211,"c":1,"h":0.016195524146054185,"t":"and are asked to name the colour of the word's font. When the colour and the","x":0.14609054193047372,"y":0.76737338054640791},{"x":0.14595298902499973,"w":0.75541742960611968,"y":0.78241100221482596,"t":"word match (i.e., the word 'red' printed in red), people correctly name the colour","c":1,"h":0.023678093271064515},{"x":0.14799676336651488,"c":1,"y":0.80207181212282797,"t":"more quickly than when the colour and the word don't match (i.e., the word 'red'","w":0.75344151814778637,"h":0.022609497268011758},{"c":1,"y":0.820066006301166,"x":0.14803009546117277,"t":"printed in blue). This occurs because the word itself ('red') and its font colour (blue)","w":0.78416737874348974,"h":0.020088989967732562},{"y":0.83816824126577771,"w":0.77592239379882799,"t":"conflict. Bilingual people often excel at tasks such as this, which tap into the ability","x":0.1480472750138988,"h":0.019952933555216656,"c":1},{"y":0.85608401644521426,"t":"to ignore competing perceptual information and focus on the relevant aspects of","h":0.019295832855260486,"x":0.14603812559474305,"w":0.75937713623046876,"c":1},{"x":0.14609052935698913,"y":0.87499999983004817,"t":"the input. Bilinguals are also better at switching between two tasks; for example,","w":0.76131688435872391,"h":0.01752061248527681,"c":1},{"x":0.14807092848321038,"y":0.89066484127400913,"t":"when bilinguals have to switch from categorizing objects by colour (red or green)","w":0.76147801717122399,"h":0.020931170882549699,"c":1},{"y":0.94476744210980945,"t":"46","x":0.10082304497181456,"h":0.014534882858869502,"w":0.026748971939086916,"c":1},{"c":1,"x":0.39483477817229695,"t":"leltsresources.org","y":0.96767833127361635,"w":0.20822526295979815,"h":0.016459778425129135}]}